the 2022 World Cup is reshaping Qatar's  landscape in three years time this  country will host football's biggest  tournaments but already plans are being  made for what happens when it's all over  the man known in Qatar is dr. cool used  to design air-conditioning for cars for  the last decade he's been developing  cooling technology for football stadiums  science with long-term value for Qatar  and other countries aiming to combat  high temperatures the air goes out  gently towards the supporters then it's  recycled again like a conveyor belt this  is one of the things that makes the  technology sustainable we did not  air-conditioned the whole stadium but  target where the spectators are it's a  unique technology that Qatar introduced  to the world creating a meaningful  legacy is a complex challenge  particularly for a small country hosting  such a huge tournament the hosts have to  make sure that not only do the stadium's  have a life beyond 2022 but that  everything else that comes with a World  Cup also has a purpose before and after  the main event it means not just elite  teams are given access to the dozens of  new training pitches that have been  built they're also home to a community  league set up by World Cup organisers in  the world has access to world cup  training facilities in three years time  we're going to have the best footballers  in the world coming here and at this  moment in time for the next three years  and after 2022 our community Football  League with all of its players will be  playing on these facilities we all come  from different backgrounds we have  different careers and we come together  and this is what joins us this year  we've played on these pitches and I get  guests coming over from from home from  the UK and I'll bring them out here it's  going to have a look at the pictures  that we're going to to play on being  being part of this and the build-up to  the World Cup and it's amazing to be  here a grassroots football culture part  of the less visible legacy organisers  hope will be in place well beyond 2022  Andy Richardson al Jazeera Doha
 

